const linkClass =
  'font-semibold text-plum underline decoration-plum/40 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-plum';

const fitSections = [
  {
    category: '1. Some measurements have no size',
    text: [
      `Read the men's waist column in order: 26–27, 28–29, 30–31, 32–34, 36–38, 40–42, 44–46, 48–50, 52–54.`,
      `The bands don't meet. M ends at 34 and L begins at 36. XL ends at 42 and XXL begins at 44. Five whole-inch waist measurements (<strong>35, 39, 43, 47 and 51</strong>) fall between two sizes and are assigned to neither.`,
      `This is not rounding. Men's waists are measured in whole inches and men's trousers are sold in whole inches, so these are real measurements belonging to real customers, sitting in the space between two rows of a chart. If you measure 35 inches, Old Navy's men's bottoms chart does not tell you what to buy.`,
      `The chest column does the same thing: <strong>43, 47 and 54 inches</strong> have no size. So does the neck, where the jump from XXXL (19–19½) to XXXXL (20½–21) leaves a 20-inch neck unassigned. Arm length leaves a half inch unassigned between every single size.`,
      `Which inches are missing depends on what you're shopping for, which is the part that should not be possible.`,
      `A 47-inch chest has no size in shirts, because L ends at 46 and XL begins at 48. In robes, where the bands are S/M 38–42 and L/XL 44–50, that same chest is an L/XL. Outerwear goes further and swaps the gaps around: its XXL runs 52–54 and its XXXL 56–58, so a 54-inch chest that has no shirt size does have an outerwear size, while 51 and 55 have shirt sizes but no outerwear size. Three men's categories, three different sets of unassigned inches, one brand.`,
      {
        ariaLabel: "Chest measurements with no size, by men's category",
        minWidth: 520,
        headers: ['Chest', 'Shirts & tees', 'Outerwear', 'Robes'],
        rows: [
          ['43', 'no size', 'no size', 'no size'],
          ['47', 'no size', 'no size', 'L/XL'],
          ['51', 'XXL', 'no size', '—'],
          ['54', 'no size', 'XXL', '—'],
          ['55', 'XXXL', 'no size', '—'],
        ],
      },
      `Women's shoes have their own version. The size ladder runs 5, 6, 6.5, 7, 7.5, 8, 8.5, 9, 9.5, 10, then jumps to 11. There is no 10.5, and the foot-length step from size 10 to size 11 is half an inch where every other step is an eighth or a quarter. A foot between 10⅜ and 10⅞ inches has no Old Navy size.`,
      `Worth knowing that the gaps are a choice, not a limitation of how size charts work. Gap, the same parent company running the same chart software for the same garment category, publishes a men's bottoms chart that runs continuously from 28 to 45 with no unassigned inches at all.`,
      `<strong>What to do:</strong> if you land in a gap, size up. The bands above a gap are wider than the bands below it, so the larger size has more room to absorb the difference, and trouser waists are easier to take in than let out.`,
    ],
