<BrandGuide
  brandKey="oldnavy"
  brand="Old Navy"
  title="Old Navy Size Chart & Fit Guide: What Their Own Charts Say"
  description="Old Navy's size charts, transcribed from the source, plus what their own numbers reveal, including the waist sizes that have no size at all."
  h1="Old Navy Size Chart & Fit Guide: What Their Own Charts Say"
  chartsHeading="The charts"
  standardCharts={false}
  typicalCharts={charts}
  fitSections={fitSections}
  faqs={faqs}
  related={related}
>
  <Fragment slot="verdict">
    <p>
      Old Navy publishes its size charts at a single address, and most of what you need to know
      about them is visible only when you compare one chart against another.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3">
      We transcribed every Old Navy chart we could reach: eight women's categories, seven men's,
      across all their fit tabs. Then we checked them against each other and against Gap's, since
      both brands belong to Gap Inc. and run the same chart software. Seven things came out of it.
      One of them means that if your waist measures 35 inches, Old Navy's men's chart does not list
      a size for you.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3">
      Everything below comes from Old Navy's own charts. Where their numbers are incomplete or
      contradict each other, we say so rather than filling the gap with a guess.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="charts-intro">
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink/85">
      Every figure below was transcribed in August 2026 from <AffLink brand="oldnavy"
        >Old Navy's own size-chart application</AffLink
      >, and the Gap comparisons from Gap's equivalent charts, both reached through the brands' own
      size-chart links. Nothing here comes from a third-party aggregator or from a reconstruction of
      these charts elsewhere.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink/85">
      All measurements are in inches, and they are body measurements rather than garment
      measurements. Old Navy's eight women's charts are drawn from one set of numbers, so the first
      table is that shared block and the second shows which rows and which range each category
      actually publishes.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="charts-outro">
    <h3 class="mt-10 text-lg font-semibold text-ink">How Old Navy tells you to measure</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ink/85">
      These are Old Navy's own instructions, from the measuring tips tab of their size-chart page.
      They publish one set for women (five measurements) and one for men (seven), and the shared
      ones are worded identically in both. Their general rule: hold the tape level with the floor,
      neither tight nor loose.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-ink/85">
      <li><strong>Chest:</strong> arms relaxed, measure around the fullest part.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Waist:</strong> bend slightly to one side to find the crease, which is your natural
        waist, and measure there.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Hips/butt:</strong> stand relaxed with feet shoulder-width apart, measure the fullest
        part.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Inseam:</strong> the inside of the leg, crotch to ankle.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Foot:</strong> stand on paper, mark behind the heel and in front of the longest toe,
        measure between the marks.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Neck</strong> (men's only): around the base of the neck.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Arm length</strong> (men's only): hand on hip, tape from the centre back of the neck
        across the shoulder, around the elbow, to the wrist.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <blockquote class="mt-5 rounded-xl border-l-4 border-plum/40 bg-white/60 px-4 py-3 text-ink/85">
      <p class="leading-relaxed">
        <strong>The waist instruction matters more than it looks.</strong> Old Navy sizes men's
        bottoms by <em>natural</em> waist, not by where you wear your jeans. The natural waist sits
        higher and measures smaller. So if you know yourself as a 34 in jeans, do not read the chart
        at 34. Measure at the crease first, because that number is what the chart is built against,
        and it will usually be lower.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-ink/85">
      Old Navy gives instructions for measuring your inseam in both divisions and publishes no
      inseam figure anywhere. It publishes high hip and underbust figures and gives no instructions
      for either. See findings 3 and 6.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink/85">
      Where Old Navy's charts are silent on inseams, on the difference between petite and tall, on
      how to measure a high hip or an underbust, or on why the plus range is graded differently,
      this guide says they're silent rather than supplying a plausible number. Where two of Old
      Navy's charts disagree, both figures are given.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink/85">
      Size charts change. This page reflects the charts as published in August 2026; we re-check
      them when we update. Our <a href="/how-to-measure/" class={linkClass}>measuring guide</a> covers
      the general method in a few minutes.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="tips">
    <li>
      <strong>If you land between two men's sizes, size up:</strong> the bands above a gap are wider
      than the bands below it, so the larger size has more room to absorb the difference, and trouser
      waists are easier to take in than let out.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Measure at your natural waist, not your jeans waist:</strong> Old Navy's charts are built
      against the higher, smaller reading, in both divisions, and their own instructions say to find
      the crease first.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Choose petite or tall from the product page's inseam, not the size chart:</strong> all
      three tabs carry identical measurements, so the tab itself tells you nothing about length.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Between sizes 14 and 20, measure rather than assume:</strong> the chart's hip-to-waist
      difference changes across exactly that stretch, from 10½ inches down to 9½.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Don't read your Gap size across:</strong> a 38-inch waist is an XL at Gap and an L at Old
      Navy, and on the women's side the two charts cross over at size 14.
    </li>
  </Fragment>
</BrandGuide>
